#!/bin/bash
# 725 (Fri 27 Nov 2026 08:42, Peter by voice: "we need to bake in a way of making money from the bigger games ... if I'm
# watching it, if we've got all our form guides together ... come up with something innovative, clever"). Adds a big-games part
# to the two football sessions that have not started yet. Paper and trial only. No orders. Nothing deleted.
B=/root/pe-builder
cat >> $B/BRIEF_small_football.md <<'EOF'

ADDED 27 Nov 08:50 ON PETER'S ORDER - THE BIG GAMES. The main market of a big televised game is the best-priced thing on the exchange and we will not beat it. Use it instead: treat it as the truth, second by second, and look at everything hanging off it. (1) THIN MARKETS INSIDE BIG GAMES: a big game has 60 market types and most have little money in them (half-time score, correct score tails, handicaps, team goals, both to score, next goal, cards, corners, player markets where recorded). From the main market price and the total goals price at each second, work out what each side market SHOULD be, and measure how often an offer in the side market is left behind by more than the cost, for how long, for how much money, before the start and above all in the 60 seconds after a goal, a red card or the half-time whistle. What would taking those have paid at £2 to £50. (2) THE CROWD AFTER A GOAL: big games bring the casual money. After a goal, a red card or a penalty in a big game, does the price go too far and come back (measure the price 10 s, 30 s, 1, 2, 5 and 10 minutes after against where it settled and against the result), and is it worse when a popular favourite concedes. As a TRADE with a target, a stop and a time limit, with the bet delay. Big game = top 10% of matches by money matched; compare against ordinary games.
EOF
cat >> $B/BRIEF_form_football.md <<'EOF'

ADDED 27 Nov 08:50 ON PETER'S ORDER - PETER'S EYES ON THE BIG GAMES. Peter will trade some games by hand and watches the big ones. A machine cannot see a tired back four or a striker limping; a man watching can, but only if he knows what to look for and only if we measure whether his eye is worth anything. Build both. (1) THE WATCH CARD: a daily job (in /root/pe-mechanic/watch.list, before the first kick-off) writes /root/pe-queue/reports/watch_card.md and pushes it: for each of the day's biggest televised games, one short block a man can read on a phone - where our form guide and the price disagree and by how much; what history says happens in games like this in play (who scores late, who folds after conceding, who comes back); and two or three plain yes/no questions to answer by watching at set moments (for example at 25 minutes and at half time), each chosen because in our data the answer moves the result more than the price moves. No trade instructions on it yet. (2) THE SCORE SHEET: table football_form.eye_calls (match, minute, question, Peter's answer, price at that second, what happened) with a small script the chat can call to record an answer, so that after 30 answered games we can say in pounds whether Peter's eye plus the card beats the price alone. Paper only until then. (3) From history, test the nearest stand-in for the eye that we do hold - shots, shots on target, expected goals and the version 3 goal signal at the set moments - to pick the questions that matter and to give a first estimate of what a correct answer is worth.
EOF
echo "725 done $(date -u +%H:%M) UTC: big-games part added to the football small-markets brief ($(grep -c 'THE BIG GAMES' $B/BRIEF_small_football.md)) and watch card to the football form brief ($(grep -c 'EYES ON THE BIG GAMES' $B/BRIEF_form_football.md)); either already started: $(pgrep -fc '[r]un_small_football.sh|[r]un_form_football.sh') (0 = both read the new briefs)." >> /root/pe-queue/reports/running_now.md
cd /root/pe-queue && git add reports/running_now.md && git commit -qm "running now: 725" -q && git pull -q --no-rebase --no-edit && env -u GITHUB_TOKEN git push -q
